  `...Label != "Second Pension starts" || got.Markers[0].Month != 9`.
No test function was deleted. I read the restructured
TestSpendingFundingBenefitTimingInflationAndCalendarScope in full: its subject
(the optimizer hook suppressing a CONFIGURED Social Security marker) is still
proved, and better — the old fixture's SS source was at month 0, which
SinceStart() now suppresses for an unrelated reason, so the worker added
"Spouse Social Security" at month 8 and "Second Pension" at month 9; the test's
single-marker assertion can only hold if the late SS source was recognised as
SS (engine.IsSocialSecurityIncomeSource) AND suppressed by the hook, while the
late pension survives. The two added sources start at months 8/9 and every
arithmetic assertion in that test reads Months[0], Months[1] and
AnnualAverages[0] (2026 = months 0-1), so none of them was perturbed or
loosened; AnnualAverages[1]/[2] carry no income figure. Mutation M3 confirms
the restructured assertion still bites.

